                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "As an initial validation, we first demonstrate the recovery of astrophysical SGWB in 2.8_EQ dataset using the template-based method. In Fig. 3, the posterior distribution is shown in blue shaded region. Both the amplitude and the power index of the injected astrophysical SGWB are successfully recovered within the 1σ credible interval (CI). This result provides evidence for the applicability of our pipeline. As note in [37], the amplitudes of individual noise components exhibit a strong degeneracy in the equal-arm orbital configuration. We define an averaged amplitude for each SC over its associated MOSAs as  $ A_i = (A_{ij} + A_{ik})/2 $. We find that it can mitigate the redundant degrees of freedom. Hence, we present the posterior distribution of noise by this averaged amplitudes in Fig. 4. The error in estimating the amplitude of low-frequency ACC noise is slightly larger, but still within the 2σ CI.",
            "block_bbox": [
                103,
                991,
                1127,
                1174
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    103.0,
                    991.0
                ],
                [
                    1127.0,
                    991.0
                ],
                [
                    1127.0,
                    1174.0
                ],
                [
                    103.0,
                    1174.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Afterwards, we will focus on resolving the SGWB in the flexing-arm scenario, putting results of noise parameters to Appendix B. In the equal-arm case, the T channel constitutes a null channel insensitive to the GW signals, making it useful for monitoring instrumental noise. However, its responses to noise and GW are significantly affected by variations of armlength [58]. We have done an attempt, that is, recovering astrophysical SGWB using an equal-arm model on flexing-arm dataset. The results show a strongly biased estimate: 95% CI for  $ A_{ap} $ is [1.66, 1.72]  $ \\times $ 10 $ ^{-12} $ and the posterior for  $ \\gamma_{ap} $ concentrated at -1, which is the lower bound of our chosen prior. This confirms that the equal-arm model is unsuitable for realistic orbit configuration. In contrast, Fig. 3 also shows the posterior distributions for astrophysical SGWB parameters after flexing-arm effect considered. The injected values are successfully recovered and the variance of armlength does not bring significant changes to the confidence contours. A similar analysis to cosmological PT SGWB is presented in Fig. 5. We have obtained meaningful constraints on the background intensity and its peak frequency, while the constraint on the parameter  $ r_{b} $ remains relatively weak. Our analysis pipeline takes about  $ \\mathcal{O}(10) $ hours using 64 cores of an AMD EPYC chip.",
            "block_bbox": [
